<body>

<h1>⚡ JavaScript Core - Master Interview Playbook</h1>
<p><em>Structured with: Concept Clarity + <strong>Exact Speaking Scripts</strong> + Interview Traps & Solutions.</em></p>

<h2>1. Execution Context & JS Engine</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong>
  JavaScript runs code in an <em>Execution Context</em> with 2 phases: 
  (1) <strong>Memory Creation Phase:</strong> variables get <span class="code">undefined</span> and functions get full body code. 
  (2) <strong>Code Execution Phase:</strong> executes line-by-line.
</div>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Interviewer Ke Samne Kya Bolna Hai (Speaking Script):</strong><br>
  "Sir, everything in JavaScript happens inside an Execution Context. When code executes, the JS Engine processes it in two distinct phases: First is the <strong>Memory Creation Phase</strong>, where memory is allocated for variables (initialized to undefined) and functions (copied in full). Second is the <strong>Code Execution Phase</strong>, where code is executed line-by-line in a single thread."
</div>

<div class="trap-box">
  <strong>⚠️ Cross-Question Trap:</strong> "What is the Call Stack?"<br>
  <strong>Answer:</strong> "Sir, the Call Stack maintains the order of execution of execution contexts. When a function is invoked, its execution context is pushed onto the stack, and popped off upon return."
</div>

<h2>2. Hoisting & Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong>
  <span class="code">var</span> is placed in Global Scope (<span class="code">window.x</span>), while <span class="code">let</span> and <span class="code">const</span> are placed in a separate <strong>Script Scope</strong>. Both are hoisted, but <span class="code">let</span> remains uninitialized inside the TDZ until its declaration line executes.
</div>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, Hoisting is a mechanism where declarations are allocated memory before execution. Both var and let/const are hoisted. However, while var is initialized with undefined in global scope, let and const remain uninitialized in a separate Script Scope. The time between a variable being hoisted and its initialization is the <strong>Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)</strong>. Accessing it inside the TDZ throws a ReferenceError."
</div>

<h3>Top 4 Errors Quick Table</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Error Type</th><th>Reason</th><th>Example</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>ReferenceError: Cannot access before initialization</strong></td><td>Variable is in <strong>TDZ</strong></td><td><span class="code">console.log(y); let y = 50;</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>ReferenceError: x is not defined</strong></td><td>Variable never declared in scope</td><td><span class="code">console.log(x);</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>SyntaxError: Identifier has already been declared</strong></td><td>Duplicate declaration of let in same scope</td><td><span class="code">let a = 10; let a = 20;</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>TypeError: Assignment to constant variable</strong></td><td>Reassigning a const variable</td><td><span class="code">const pi = 3.14; pi = 4;</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3. Lexical Environment & Closures</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
  <strong>🧠 Dimag Me Samajhne Ke Liye:</strong>
  Child function always has access to parent and global variables even after the parent function has finished executing and returned from call stack.
</div>

<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, a <strong>Closure</strong> is the combination of a function bundled together with references to its lexical environment. In simple terms, an inner function always retains access to the variables of its outer parent function, even after the parent function has finished executing and returned from the call stack."
</div>

<pre>function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // Private variable
  return function() {
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}
const counter = createCounter();
console.log(counter()); // 1
console.log(counter()); // 2</pre>

<h2>4. The Loop + setTimeout Trap</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "When we use <span class="code">var</span> inside a for loop with setTimeout, all callbacks close over the same shared global/function scoped variable <span class="code">i</span>. By the time the timer expires, the loop has completed and <span class="code">i</span> has become 4, so it prints 4, 4, 4. To fix this, we replace var with <span class="code">let</span>, which creates a new block scope for each loop iteration."
</div>

<h2>5. Debouncing (Performance Optimization)</h2>
<div class="script-box">
  <strong>🗣️ Speaking Script:</strong><br>
  "Sir, Debouncing is a performance optimization technique that limits the rate at which a function triggers. It guarantees that the function executes only after a specified quiet period (e.g. 300ms) has elapsed since the user last fired the event."
</div>

<pre>function debounce(fn, delay = 300) {
  let timerId = null;

  return function (...args) {
    if (timerId) clearTimeout(timerId); // Cancel previous timer

    timerId = setTimeout(() => {
      fn.apply(this, args);
    }, delay);
  };
}</pre>

<h2>6. Array Methods & reduce Empty Array Trap</h2>
<div class="trap-box">
  <strong>⚠️ Interview Trap:</strong> Calling reduce on an empty array without an initialValue throws:
  <strong><span class="code">TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value</span></strong>. Always pass initialValue!
</div>

<pre>const fruits = ['apple', 'banana', 'apple'];
const counts = fruits.reduce((acc, f) => {
  acc[f] = (acc[f] || 0) + 1;
  return acc;
}, {});
// Result: { apple: 2, banana: 1 }</pre>

